@page "/toolings/blazor-es2019"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="toolings/blazor-es2019"
            Title="bit BlazorES2019 - Toolings"
            Description="bit BlazorES2019 builds Blazor's own scripts for ES2019, so Blazor apps also run on older iPhones, Macs and Android WebViews that the stock scripts leave with a blank page." />

<div class="page-container">
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H3" Element="h1" Gutter>bit BlazorES2019</BitText>
    <br />
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Blazor for the devices people really have</BitText>
    <br />
    <BitText Typography="BitTypography.Subtitle1" Gutter>
        Not everyone has this year's phone. Many people keep a device for years, and many of those devices can't update their browser any more.
        We care that apps built with bit work for them too, so bit BlazorES2019 brings Blazor back to the older iPhones, Macs and Android devices
        that its own scripts leave behind.
    </BitText>
    <CodeBox>dotnet add package Bit.BlazorES2019</CodeBox>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>The problem</BitText>
            .NET itself runs on Safari 15, Chrome and Edge 95, Firefox 100, iOS 15, macOS 12 and Android 8 with WebView 84.
            Blazor's own scripts, <code>blazor.web.js</code>, <code>blazor.webassembly.js</code>, <code>blazor.server.js</code> and <code>blazor.webview.js</code>,
            are compiled for ES2022 and newer (ES2024 in .NET 10), which raises the bar:
            <div class="table-container">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th></th><th>.NET needs</th><th>Blazor's scripts need</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Safari</td><td>15</td><td>16.4</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>iOS</td><td>15</td><td>16.4</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Android WebView</td><td>84</td><td>94</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <br />
            So an iPhone that stopped at iOS 15 shows a blank page, although .NET runs on it just fine.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>The fix</BitText>
            bit BlazorES2019 is the same Blazor scripts, built from the source of the official ASP.NET Core repository with only the compile target changed to ES2019.
            Our release pipelines build them for every bit release, for .NET 9 and .NET 10, so nothing else about Blazor changes.
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>How to use it</BitText>
            <b>1.</b> Install the package in the project that serves Blazor's script: the server project of a Blazor Web App,
            the project of a standalone Blazor WebAssembly app, or the MAUI project of a Blazor Hybrid app.
            <CodeBox>dotnet add package Bit.BlazorES2019</CodeBox>
            <b>2.</b> Load its script from <code>_framework/</code> instead of Blazor's own:
            <ul>
                <li><code>bit.blazor.web.es2019.js</code> instead of <code>blazor.web.js</code></li>
                <li><code>bit.blazor.webassembly.es2019.js</code> instead of <code>blazor.webassembly.js</code></li>
                <li><code>bit.blazor.server.es2019.js</code> instead of <code>blazor.server.js</code></li>
                <li><code>bit.blazor.webview.es2019.js</code> instead of <code>blazor.webview.js</code></li>
            </ul>
            <CodeBox>&lt;script src="_framework/bit.blazor.web.es2019.js">&lt;/script></CodeBox>
            <b>3.</b> For Blazor WebAssembly on those older browsers, also turn off WebAssembly SIMD in the client project:
            <CodeBox>&lt;PropertyGroup>
    &lt;WasmEnableSIMD>false&lt;/WasmEnableSIMD>
&lt;/PropertyGroup></CodeBox>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-card">
        <div class="section-card-txt">
            <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H5" Gutter>Older devices across bit</BitText>
            bit Boilerplate loads these scripts out of the box, and the JavaScript that bit BlazorUI, Bswup, Butil and the utilities ship is built for ES2019 as well,
            so an app made with bit reaches the same devices from end to end.
            The script that builds them is in the <a href="https://github.com/bitfoundation/bitplatform/tree/develop/src/Toolings/BlazorES2019" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bit BlazorES2019's README</a>.
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

<NavigationButtons Prev="Minifier" PrevUrl="@Urls.Minifier" Next="Code Analyzers" NextUrl="@Urls.CodeAnalyzers" />
